<LabLayout title="Expeditions: model & research · Tether Lab" description="How the persistent Earth–Moon–Phobos campaign works, what it calculates, and which assumptions belong to the game." active="campaign">
  <main id="lab-content" class="guide">
    <header class="guide-header"><p class="micro">EXPEDITIONS / MODEL & RESEARCH</p><h1>A network worth coming back to.</h1><p>Begin with an Earth depot. Deliver construction cargo to the Moon, commission a lunavator, and build a Phobos-anchored transport hub.</p><div class="guide-meta"><span>CAMPAIGN MODEL 0.2.0</span><span>LOCAL SAVES + BACKUPS</span><span>STRATEGIC LOGISTICS</span></div><a class="button-link primary" href="/lab/campaign/">Open your expeditions</a></header>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>What you can do now</h2><p>Use bootstrap tugs to deliver cargo to unbuilt depots. Thirty tonnes of delivered construction cargo commissions a tier-one facility. When both ends have a tether, book the corridor service. Its capacity is limited by the lower-rated endpoint. Upgrade facilities using materials delivered to their own depots.</p><p>Earth–Moon, Earth–Phobos and direct Moon–Phobos corridors support travel in both directions. Construction material and equipment are separate cargo types, each stored at its destination. Each delivery is credited exactly once. Construction consumes cargo; launching reserves cargo and support propellant immediately. Earth supplies remain available every 30 simulation days, so an empty depot is recoverable.</p><p>Advance by a day, 30 days, or the next event; or press Play at 1, 10 or 30 simulation days per second. Production, arrivals and scheduled departures are processed in time order even during a large jump. Hiding or closing the tab pauses the campaign. Reloading, changing worlds and a failed save also stop automatic play. There is no offline progress or real-time waiting requirement.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Give each destination a job.</h2><p>Earth starts with manufacturing, 20 t of equipment, and its existing construction and propellant stocks. Manufacturing contributes 0.5 t of equipment and 1 t of pooled support propellant per simulation day. Earth-surface resources and delivery into the network are abstracted.</p><p>Install a lunar processor or a Phobos staging depot for 20 t construction material and 5 t equipment at that location, after commissioning its tether. The processor produces 1 t construction material per day and consumes 0.05 t equipment per day as maintenance. The staging depot consumes 0.5 t construction material and 0.02 t equipment per day to earn one Mars operations point. An operations point is an abstract measure of supported activity, not a simulated surface mission or scientific quantity.</p><p>Production stops when an input is exhausted or output storage is full. Inputs are consumed only for completed production. Incoming deliveries reserve storage; each resource has a limit of one million tonnes per depot. Industry has a single production tier in this chapter.</p><p>For a working supply loop, schedule 5 t equipment from Earth to the Moon every 90 days, 3 t equipment from Earth to Phobos every 100 days, and 10 t lunar construction material to Phobos every 20 days. Both remote industries need their initial construction and equipment deliveries first. The first Phobos deliveries take months of simulation time.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Services that wait for their cargo.</h2><p>Use the flight form to choose origin, destination, cargo type, payload and transport, then schedule an interval from 1 to 3,650 days. The first attempt is the next simulation day. A successful departure schedules the next one after that interval. If supplies, endpoint capacity, recovery, propellant, storage or traffic limits block a departure, the service tries again one day later. Missed departures do not accumulate.</p><p>Up to twelve services can share a network. At the same instant, deliveries arrive before service bookings, and older service IDs get priority. Every departure uses the same cargo, fuel, capacity and recovery rules as a manual flight. Pause or remove a service to stop new attempts; flights already in transit still arrive. Resuming schedules the next attempt no earlier than tomorrow. Next event includes service attempts, arrivals, recovery and supply availability.</p><p>Track a flight from the traffic list to highlight it on the schematic map. The display shows its destination and remaining time, and reports its delivery. Marker position is elapsed fraction along a diagram line, not a spacecraft position.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="lunavator"><h2>The Moon has its own machine.</h2><p>A lunavator is a rotating tether in lunar orbit. It is not attached to the lunar surface. The historical Cislunar Tether Transport System studies paired an Earth tether with a lunar rotor to exchange cargo with the Moon; lunar pickup and delivery depend on the tether's orbit, spin, and rendezvous geometry.</p><p>In this chapter, the lunavator is a constructible transport facility with a finite service rating and recovery schedule. Its surface pickup, flexible dynamics, capture shock, and orbital momentum exchange are not numerically integrated. An operational game route is not a validated reproduction of the historical design.</p><p><a href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/7Hoyt.pdf">Hoyt, Cislunar Tether Transport System, NIAC Phase I final report (1999)</a> — project summary, section III.A.3 and Appendix B.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="phobos"><h2>Phobos is the central anchor.</h2><p>The campaign's architecture has an inward tether facing Mars and another extending outward from Phobos. The moon itself anchors the structure. This is distinct from the free rotor used at Earth and from the lunar lunavator.</p><p>This is a logistics representation of a proposed architecture. The model does not calculate anchor strength, excavation, tether tension, flexible motion, or Mars-surface rendezvous. Arrival at the Phobos depot is a scheduled handling event; it is not a solved interception or landing trajectory.</p><p><a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20030065879">Weinstein, Space Colonization Using Space-Elevators from Phobos (2003)</a> — conceptual architecture reference, not a hardware qualification.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Transfer times have a physical basis.</h2><p>Coast time is calculated as half the period of an ideal transfer ellipse: π × √(a³ / μ), where a is the average of the departure and arrival orbital radii. This is a two-body, circular-orbit estimate. It does not use live planetary positions, launch windows, inclination changes, finite burns, or three-body capture dynamics.</p><ul><li>Earth–Moon: Earth gravitational parameter 3.986004418 × 10¹⁴ m³/s², Earth radius 6,371 km, departure altitude 1,600 km, and lunar orbital radius 384,400 km. Coast estimate: {ROUTES[0].coastDays.toFixed(2)} days, plus one scenario day for handling.</li><li>Earth–Phobos: solar gravitational parameter 1.32712440018 × 10²⁰ m³/s², Earth at 1 AU, and Mars at 1.523679 AU. Coast estimate: {ROUTES[1].coastDays.toFixed(2)} days, plus three scenario days for Mars-system handling. Phobos interception is not calculated.</li><li>Moon–Phobos: the same heliocentric coast estimate, approximating the Moon at Earth’s solar orbital radius, plus four scenario days for departure and arrival handling. It does not calculate escape from lunar orbit or an encounter with Phobos. Reverse routes use the same estimates.</li></ul><p><a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/astro_par.html">JPL astrodynamic parameters</a> and <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/approx_pos.html">approximate planetary elements</a> provide the reference context. The scenario deliberately freezes its constants; these are not ephemerides.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Game assumptions, openly stated</h2><p>These values define the playable network. They are balancing choices, not engineering predictions:</p><ul><li>Construction: 30 / 60 / 90 t for tiers 1 / 2 / 3, built immediately.</li><li>Service rating: 10 / 20 / 30 t per tether flight; bootstrap tugs carry 10 t.</li><li>Recovery: a network booking reserves both endpoint services for 2 / tier simulation days. It is a simplified scheduling rule, including the destination reservation, not an arrival-time rendezvous solver.</li><li>Support propellant: 0.35 t per cargo tonne for cislunar tugs and 1.2 t per cargo tonne for either Mars corridor’s tugs. Tether bookings use 40% of these allocations. This is a network budget for assumed targeting, handling and recovery support; no rocket equation or quantified historical tether fuel saving is implied.</li><li>Supply: 60 t construction cargo at Earth and 60 t support propellant per allocation, available every 30 days. Support propellant is pooled across the network; its own delivery is abstracted.</li><li>Up to 32 active flights, 12 save slots and 100,000 simulation days per campaign.</li></ul><p>The <a href="/lab/">Flight Studio</a> remains a separate, more detailed Earth-only educational simulation. Campaign facility ratings are not imported from a Flight Studio design, and an Earth run is not evidence that a lunar or Phobos transfer closes.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Saves that protect your progress</h2><p>Every successful action, including an automatic time step, attempts a browser save. The screen reports success only after the database transaction completes. Each named campaign retains its three preceding checkpoints. Recovering a checkpoint or importing a backup creates a separate slot, preserving the original.</p><p>Saves belong to this browser and site address. A preview address and the main website have separate storage. Download a backup before switching devices, changing site addresses, using private browsing, or clearing browser data. Browser storage can also be removed under storage pressure. There is no account or cloud synchronisation in this chapter.</p><p>If two tabs edit the same save, the older tab is prevented from overwriting newer progress. Keep an export of the older tab if you want that branch, then load the latest saved world. First-chapter saves (schema 1, network-0.1.0) upgrade compatibly when loaded: simulation day, existing stocks, facility tiers, flights and arrival dates, logs and objectives are preserved. Earth receives the new manufacturing facility and a one-time 20 t equipment allocation; no past production is added. The first write retains the old state as a recovery checkpoint and advances its revision, including when using Save now. Existing version-one backups still import into new slots. New exports use version two. Unrecognised versions are rejected without replacing existing campaigns.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Beyond the first corridors</h2><p>Mercury mining, mirror manufacture, launch infrastructure and a solar swarm are later chapters. They are not unlocked by completing this chapter yet. The specific mirror material and production chain remain research questions. The working network now includes production, scheduled services and Mars staging objectives alongside recoverable saves.</p><a class="button-link primary" href="/lab/campaign/">Return to your network</a></section>
